`karr create` takes `--class` and `--estimate`; `karr edit` takes neither, and the only way to clear a due date is to rewrite the card by hand (materialize, edit the file, import). kanban-md's edit has `--class`, `--estimate`, `--clear-due`.

The class matters most: `karr pick` orders candidates by class of service before priority (expedite > fixed-date > standard > intangible, k233), so a card that turns out to be an expedite after creation cannot be promoted, and a fixed-date card whose date went away cannot drop back.

## What to build

- `edit --class CLASS`, validated against the board's classes with the same message and exit 2 as create.
- `edit --estimate TEXT`.
- `edit --clear-due` (usage error together with `--due`).
- kanban-md additionally has `--started/--clear-started` and `--completed/--clear-completed`; out of scope unless they fall out of the same code path. Note the decision at handoff.

The batch form `edit 1,2,3 --class expedite` follows from Role::TaskMutation; make sure the activity log records the field. Changes entry, tests.

Pointers: Task.pm already carries due and estimate as rw attributes with predicate and clearer, and class with a default of standard, so no card-format change is needed. Cmd::Create validates class through Config->validate_class (around line 202); Cmd::Edit's option validation gets the same call. The field writes go through Role::TaskMutation like --priority does, which is what puts them in the activity log. Follow Cmd::Edit's existing --due handling for the pattern and the batch behaviour.

